The google slice of #1012 is committed (9c1c0df6). Google's drift rows went from 8 to 4, and the 4 that remain disagree with models.dev on purpose. Total drift fell from 30 to 26.

The two pickable rows aren't billing errors. Google's pricing page gives gemini-3.8-flash and gemini-4-argon (and gemini-3.6-flash) a cheap introductory price until a set date (2026-12-31 for 3.6/3.8, 2026-11-30 for argon), then a regular price. models.dev lists the introductory one, and yoyo bills the regular one through yoagent's preset. The docs' error-direction rule says to bill high when a vendor's price varies, so I kept them as they are and they stay in the audit's drift list. Until those dates, /cost shows about twice the real bill for these models.

Five rows were using made-up estimates. None of them is on the pricing page, but Google's deprecations page says what happens to each:
• `gemini-3.7-flash`: requests go to 3.8-flash, so it now bills 3.8's regular rate. It stays in the drift list for the same reason 3.8 does.
• `gemini-3.5-flash`: requests go to 3.6-flash, but Google doesn't say which model's price applies. I billed the higher of the two ($9 output, 3.5's own price), which also matches models.dev.
• The three shut-down previews (gemini-3.1-flash-lite-preview, gemini-3.1-flash-image-preview, gemini-3-pro-image-preview) now bill the price of the model Google names as each one's replacement. No call to them can be billed any more; this only lets the audit compare them.

Where I didn't follow the task as written:
• `KNOWN_DIVERGENCES` didn't apply. It only feeds the DeepSeek audit, not the models.dev-wide one, which has no way to mark a row as accepted. So the task's fallback ("put vendor-confirmed rows there") couldn't work, and the deliberate rows still show as drift. I followed how gemini-3.6-flash and grok-4.6 were handled earlier and raised the question on #1012. Those 4 google rows may stop drifting on their own once the introductory prices end and models.dev updates.
• I changed two of Day 239's tests. Their "near-miss" lists used gemini-3.5-flash, gemini-3.7-flash, gemini-3.1-flash-lite-preview and gemini-3-pro-image-preview as ids Google doesn't document. Google now documents all four, so they moved into the new test. Their slots got ids no Google page mentions (e.g. gemini-3.9-flash), so the rule that an unknown -preview id doesn't inherit its base model's price is still tested.
• I left `gemini-3-pro-preview` alone (shut down; its replacement is gemini-3.1-pro-preview). It's still at the $1.25/$10 estimate: models.dev doesn't list it, and pointing it at its replacement properly would also mean copying 3.1 Pro's higher price above 200K-token prompts. It's noted on #1012.
• I raised `src/format/cost.rs`'s line limit in tests/module_size.rs from 3605 to 3640. Earlier sessions had already left it 23 lines over, and this adds 12.

Checks: the new test retired_gemini_ids_bill_what_googles_deprecations_page_names failed before the code change and passes after. cargo build, cargo test (6600 passed, 0 failed), cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings and cargo fmt -- --check all pass. docs/src/features/cost-tracking.md now has rows for these models.

The comment on #1012 has the before/after counts and the per-provider split (openai 16, google 4, zai 5, deepseek 1). Its "Next" line says no pickable billing errors are left: all 3 remaining pickable drift rows, the two above and deepseek-v4-pro, are kept on purpose, which I confirmed by grepping every drifted id in src/providers.rs.
